<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fusagasugá</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fusagasugá is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Cundinamarca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> on the slopes of the Andes. Before the Spanish conquest it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of the Sutagao people on the edge of Muisca territory, conquered in the late fifteenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguamanchica">Saguamanchica</a>, zipa of Bacatá. Today it is a regional centre known for its mild climate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fusagasuga.jpg" alt="Fusagasugá" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfair · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FusagasugaCity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fusagasugá, often shortened to Fusa, lies in the warm valley between the rivers Cuja and Panches, in a central region of the Andes, about 56 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>. It stands 1,728 metres above sea level, with an average temperature of about 20 °C, and the municipality covers roughly 194 square kilometres. The name is said to come from a Cariban word meaning woman who becomes invisible; Spanish interpretations range from the woman who becomes invisible to the woman who hides herself behind the mountain. The city has been called Tierra Grata for its country houses, and the Garden City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> for its production of orchids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Sutagao people lived in the region until the Oídor Bernardino Albornoz founded the town between 5 and 13 February 1592. Not much is known about its earlier indigenous residents. At the visit of the Oídor Ibarra 759 indigenous people lived in Fusagasugá, but when the Oídor Aróstequi arrived in February 1760 only 85 remained, alongside 644 new settlers in 109 families. In 1776 two officials, Moreno and Escandón, decreed that the native villages of Pandi and Tibacuy no longer existed, and merged them into Pasca. The town became part of the province of Tequendama in 1852 and in 1895 was made capital of the new Sumapaz Province.</p><p>Roads shaped its growth. A directive of 7 August 1846 recognised the road from Fusagasugá to the southern provinces nationally, and an iron and wood bridge over the Sumapaz River at Boquerón was finished in 1907. A highway from Sibaté, begun in 1905 and interrupted until 1913, reached Fusagasugá in 1930. On 9 February 1877 government forces fought rebels at the hacienda El Novillero, and the first hospital was built in 1893. The main church has been rebuilt several times, from the first church of June 1658 to the fourth, begun in June 1909 and consecrated on 15 August 1926. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a hotel in the city held citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> countries as an internment camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality had a population of 138,498 at the 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, of whom 114,722 lived in the urban centre. Its economy rests mainly on agriculture and construction, with a growing service sector in education, health, recreation and tourism. Several universities have campuses there, including the Universidad de Cundinamarca. The Pan-American highway passes through the municipality, and travellers from the north bound for Ibagué, Neiva or Cali must pass through it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fusagasugá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In their accounts the zipas of Bacatá expanded their domain from the later fifteenth century: Saguamanchica took <b>Fusagasugá</b>, and his successor Nemequene subdued Guatavita, Ubaque and other chiefdoms and fought the zaques of Hunza.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Muisca tradition names Meicuchuca as an early zipa, and Saguamanchica expanded the realm by conquering neighbouring chiefdoms such as <b>Fusagasugá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/zipazgo-of-bacata">Zipazgo of Bacatá</a></li></ul></section>
